{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 62, :comments ["<b>coming from the approach of Be'er Lachai Ro'i</b> — it says that Isaac was now coming from the approach of Be'er Lachai Ro'i, for he was returning from Be'er Lachai, to which he had gone. For had it said \"coming from Be'er Lachai Ro'i,\" it would have appeared that he dwelt there. Therefore it was necessary to explain that he was returning to his city from his going to Be'er Lachai Ro'i for a short while, for he dwells in the land of the Negev and returns to his city.\n\nAnd it is possible that because מִבּוֹא is a construct form (מקור), Isaac would regularly go to that place, for it is a place of prayer for him because the angel appeared there, and he dwells in the land of the Negev near there. And thus Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Genesis 24:62) אֲתָא מִמֵּתוֹהִי — and according to his opinion it is Be'er-sheva, for he translated \"between Kadesh and Shur\" (Genesis 20:1) and \"between Kadesh and Bered\" (Genesis 16:14) as רְקַם וְחַגְרָא, and if so, that place is the place of Abraham's tamarisk tree, fitting for prayer.\n\nNow Isaac was coming from that well to another city that was on the way to his city, and he went out toward evening to stroll in the field with his companions and his friends who were there, and he encountered the servant and Rebekah, and all of them went together to his city, and he brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:33.455213979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}